<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="acetyl-CoA biosynthesis from citrate" organism="ARA" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5172" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Acetyl-CoA is a central metabolite that plays important roles in many aspects of metabolism. In plants, like in animals, mitochondrial acetyl-CoA feeds into the TCA cycle for the generation of energy and reducing power. In the plastid, acetyl-CoA serves as a substrate for de novo fatty acid biosynthesis. In the plant cell cytosol, acetyl-CoA (its derivative malonyl-CoA to be precise) is a precursor for the synthesis of very long chain fatty acids and many secondary metabolites including flavonoids and terpenoids. The distinct pools of acetyl-CoA are generated via different biosynthetic routes in plants. The mitochondrial and plastid acetyl-CoA pools are derived from pyruvate by pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (see pathway |FRAME: PYRUVDEHYD-PWY|), whereas the cytosolic pool is derived from |FRAME: CIT| in a reaction catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.3.3.8|. Besides plants, the pathway for acetyl-CoA biosynthesis from cirate, illustrated here, also occurs in vertebrates. In vertebrates, de novo fatty acid biosynthesis and cholesterol genesis are cytosolic processes. The acetyl-CoA synthesized from citrate in the cytosol feeds into these processes.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
